6134017 #edb/ref 1045 "Periodic Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134017 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186134881 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134017 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134017 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134017 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "1129f5af-8b20-5759-9f3e-97b087634b1b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134881 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134882 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134881 #edb/ref 1061 3664 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134881 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fa1d756d-c380-5339-a443-11ba085cc909" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134881 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134882 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134883 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134882 #edb/ref 1061 3665 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134882 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5c25e487-4990-59fc-bfc4-1a9c9ad30723" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134882 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134883 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134883 #edb/ref 1061 3668 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134883 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e726ab95-f076-53ce-a8d3-da7938f96dec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134883 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133903 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134884 #edb/ref 1061 3671 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134884 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "19fd2e5e-dcb6-5935-bb5a-e6645996a5d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134884 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135013 #edb/ref 1064 "**Periodic functions** are special functions that repeat themselves every ![](images/b3/b332ba26d1b8a3e4b8f817ed7eed0f807c5cc8457a0f29182ba04d25cbcb41b7.svg) units. The smallest positive value of ![](images/b3/b332ba26d1b8a3e4b8f817ed7eed0f807c5cc8457a0f29182ba04d25cbcb41b7.svg) that has this property is called the **period** of the function. \n\nLet's take the following periodic function as an example:\n\n![](images/c8/c8012ef5d26d04c81a6200bffb85babe8787c49258f5c014e6ac42ccbbbbf3cb.png)\n\nThis function repeats itself with a period of ![](images/27/270d33581e0657a10d567c6cfaa529521b34e20a39c9a29550698cbf252fabfa.svg) To illustrate, first focus on the part of the graph shown in blue below.\n\n![](images/c3/c309cafb184c76a6630f4357e07267ad832e85dd982fffce3abd28bf1526ec95.png)\n\nIf we shift this part ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) units to the right, the graph remains unchanged!\n\n![](images/a8/a82b5b1fd4da072d954d19bae0a790e82704343ec8b9d4448076f9daebe90617.png)\n\nThe same would happen if we shifted the part ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) units to the left. \n\nBy knowing the period of a periodic function, we can use a value of the function to infer many other values of the function. For example, in the function above, note that ![](images/4b/4ba0fb2ba36b90b8993c542124873b19eb240bed743d3c7bce2712f043187c4e.svg) Since the period is ![](images/0b/0be337b1b7891cff68331fbd1be3c8eb739eb8f47e7788e487ab91a8b2c88b67.svg) if we add (or subtract) any multiple of ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) from the input, the output stays the same. We can write this as\n\n![](images/b3/b3d30eb46010a29136217b3d004e0878a8923a94e77a52192172638697b6c67a.svg)\n\nwhere ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) is an integer. \n\nFor example, setting ![](images/a4/a44568f6fc5aa5bd466f76f6220d308431c1187631cf36f8bebf2290ef7a0d83.svg) gives the following:\n\n![](images/1e/1e73284bd11385d40e11887964fa4ad1a7f523982c885c5fca7f87197ea1c954.svg)\n\nThe same is true for negative values of ![](images/c2/c2475d72e6ec36042292de6c0160930174172b2386148d823a70e59fddfb93c2.svg) Setting ![](images/55/55af3f37f9d82f0e1d9e6a77096eba247f13ea48ea005c2fd0b8719f1d6c04c1.svg) gives the following:\n\n![](images/7d/7df85879c234dbc914f4f4ee9261f4e12f0a57b03ae7ab433ddd0c8c4ba14d7f.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135013 #edb/ref 1065 1502 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135013 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b8020c2e-cfe4-5187-94df-23d7d0fcb605" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135013 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234038 #edb/ref 1069 "q-41478" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234038 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234038 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/34/34ba4a4dcd6655d68fbc20fb6192354ae755cc7eaf14a2459e9b7012ffe6d9ea.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Given that $y=f(x)$ is periodic with period $4,$ what is the value of $f(62)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234038 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1a341a11-ce8b-58fe-beef-6c590edc7b20" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234038 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the period is $4,$ if we add (or subtract) any multiple of $4$ from the input, the output stays the same.\n\nSo, we can keep subtracting $4$ from the input until we get to an input that we see in the graph:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(62) & =f(62-4) \\\\  & =f(62-2\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =⋯ \\\\  & =f(62-15\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =f(2) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234131 #edb/ref 1069 "q-51567" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234131 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234131 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/45/4585ab21f7130a45e2ff5871e0fdc66cda5483181033010519781d9725eec36a.png)\n\nThe graph of a periodic function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. What is the period of this function?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234131 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "44e22570-10cc-598d-a717-a67ff911634b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234131 #edb/ref 1074 "Note that if we shift the graph $3$ units left or right, the graph remains unchanged.\n\nOn the other hand, if we shift the graph horizontally by any distance other than $3$ (or a multiple of $3$), we get a different graph.\n\nTherefore, the period of the function is $3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
